I've been a writer all my life and I've  run with shoes pretty much all that time  but as a result of this research I  thought I should try barefoot running  and I actually have to say I really  enjoyed it it's been a lot of fun humans  have been running for at least two  million years and of course for most of  that time humans were running barefoot  and modern running shoes were actually  only invented in the mid-1970s so we  have this idea now that you know in  order to run you need you need you know  all you need a pair of shoes it's a  common statement right well actually  that's not true you don't need shoes you  just need feet there's probably two  stages in the evolution of the foot  initially the foot evolved for walking  and also to climb trees but at some  point in human evolution we think around  2 million years ago there was a big  environmental change in Africa and the  woodlands started disappearing and the  savannas started growing and at that  point new foods started appearing and  one of them of course was was meat for  all these ungulates out there on the  grasslands and in order to become a  hunter I think humans started to evolve  running and what we're good at is it  running it running at speeds that make  animals gallop and if you do that in the  heat for a long period of time that  animal will overheat because quadrupeds  cannot pant and gallop at the same time  so imagine you're chasing a gazelle or a  kudu or some big animal if you can chase  that animal make that animal gallop for  10 to 15 minutes you've got dinner  we wanted to figure out how people ran  without shoes before the shoe was  invented because people have been  running for millions of years and we're  really we weren't really sure what  happens when when barefoot runners run  and how well they can they can do it so  we started bringing in habitual barefoot  runners into the lab to see just to see  how they're they use their bodies and  how they use their feet all right here  